qmail-qfilter(8)            System Manager's Manual           qmail-qfilter(8)



NNAAMMEE
       qmail-qfilter - front end for qmail-queue that does filtering

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr _f_i_l_t_e_r [ _-_- _f_i_l_t_e_r _._._.  ]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr  acts  as  a qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee frontend to execute message fil‐
       ters. It is invoked by  setting  the  QQMMAAIILLQQUUEEUUEE  environment  variable
       qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr  sends  the  message text through each of the filter com‐
       mands named on the command line and in the _q_f_i_l_t_e_r_s control  file  (al‐
       lowing  finer  control, see below). Each filter is run separately, with
       standard input opened to the input email, and standard output opened to
       a new temporary file that will become the input to either the next fil‐
       ter, or qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee. If the filter  does  not  modify  the  message  it
       passes  unchanged  to the next step.  It also makes the envelope avail‐
       able to each filter as file descriptor 3. File descriptor 4  is  opened
       to  a new temporary file for the modified envelope, allowing the filter
       to modify the envelope or the message. If the filter  does  not  modify
       the envelope, the envelope remains unchanged for either the next filter
       or qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee. This provides compatibility for existing  filters  that
       do  not  know  about the envelope. qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr also opens up file de‐
       scriptor 5 to a temporary file. If this file is  empty  after  all  the
       filters have executed, its contents are read and used to specify a pro‐
       gram to execute in place of qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee. Each  filter  on  the  command
       line is separated by ----.


RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
       Returns 51 (out of memory), 53 (write error), or 81 (internal error) if
       it can't create the temporary files or has problems executing the  fil‐
       ters.

       Returns  79  (bad envelope data) if it can't read or parse the envelope
       data.

       qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr returns exit code of filter, if the filter  returns  any‐
       thing  other  than  0  or 99. If your filter exits 88, you can put your
       custom error string on standard error. This enables client like  qqmmaaiill--
       ssmmttppdd to report the error. The custom error string can be prefixed with
       DD for permanent error and ZZ to indicate temporary error.  If  a  filter
       returns  99,  qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr  returns  0 immediately without running any
       other filters. If your filter returns 31, the mail will be  permanently
       rejected.  If  your filter returns 71, the mail will be temporarily re‐
       jected.


CCOONNTTRROOLL FFIILLEESS
       _q_f_i_l_t_e_r_s
            This control file can be used to setup additional  filters.  Lines
            in this file have the following format:

            ddoommaaiinn::ffiilltteerr
              or
            ddoommaaiinn::ffiilltteerr::eennvvssttrr

            _d_o_m_a_i_n  refers  to  the envelope sender domain. It can be an exact
            match, wildcard or a regular  expression  (if  QREGEX  environment
            variable  is defined).  If there are multiple lines that match _d_o_‐
            _m_a_i_n, all matching filters will be applied.

            _f_i_l_t_e_r is the filter command to run (passed through /bin/sh).

            _e_n_v_s_t_r mentioned above is a string of comma-separated  environment
            variable additions or removals.

            Example entry:

            *:/var/qmail/bin/surbfilter:CACHELIFTIME=,SURBL_DEBUG_FN=/dev/null


EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       For  compatibility with previous versions, qqmmaaiill--qqffiilltteerr sets QQMMAAIILLUUSSEERR
       and QQMMAAIILLHHOOSSTT to the user and host portions of the envelope sender  ad‐
       dress, and unsets QQMMAAIILLNNAAMMEE. It also sets QQMMAAIILLRRCCPPTTSS to the list of en‐
       velope recipients, each followed by a newline.

       It also sets EENNVVSSIIZZEE to the size of the envelope, MMSSGGSSIIZZEE to the length
       of the message, and NNUUMMRRCCPPTTSS to the number of recipients.  These values
       are updated before each filter is run.

       If QQQQFF__QQMMAAIILLQQUUEEUUEE is set, that value is used in place of qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee.


SSEEEE AALLSSOO
       qmail-queue(8), spawn-filter(8)


NNOOTTEESS
       $$QQMMAAIILLQQUUEEUUEE is deliberately not used to override qqmmaaiill--qquueeuuee  in  order
       to avoid recursive loops with configurations that set $$QQMMAAIILLQQUUEEUUEE.


WWAARRNNIINNGGSS
       If you are using qqmmaaiill--iinnjjeecctt -n as one of the filters, you may want to
       unset MMAAIILLUUSSEERR, UUSSEERR, and LLOOGGNNAAMMEE by using

       eennvv --uu QQMMAAIILLNNAAMMEE --uu MMAAIILLNNAAMMEE --uu NNAAMMEE qqmmaaiill--iinnjjeecctt --nn

       as the command to invoke qmail-inject. Note that some the  eennvv  command
       with some OS's doesn't support the --uu option.

       A  message  with an excessive number of recipients (more than 64K bytes
       of recipient data on Linux) will cause execution of the filter programs
       to fail, and for the message to be rejected.

       The  same  temporary file is reused for file descriptor 5 for each fil‐
       ter.  Make sure each filter writes a trailing ASCII NUL byte  following
       the  program  name,  as  multiple filters could otherwise overwrite the
       value in undesireable ways.



